          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em left-strength
            and a
            %em right-strength
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
            relating the monad with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of the category “in one direction”. A monad equipped with a (left/right) strength is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em (left/right) strong monad
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %em strengths
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A strong monad over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
                of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegory"} actegories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                (acting on itself from the left for a left-strength, and from the right for a right-strength).
            %li
              %p
                A left-strength for a monad is a compatibility condition between the monad and the tensor product “from the left”, less symmetric than being a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monad"} monoidal monad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutativity
                property. A right-strength is the analogous notion, where the action is from the right.
            %li
              %p A left-strength for a monad is a way of creating “slices”, expressions over tensor products which are trivial in the first component. (The right-strength gives expressions which are trivial in the second component.)
            %li
              %p
                A strong monad is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+strength"} strong endofunctor
                with a monad structure which is compatible with the strength.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+closed+category"} monoidal closed category
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+monad"} enriched monad
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+homs"} internal homs
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                is canonically left-strong.
          %p
            Under some conditions, having a strength is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
          %p
            A more general notion of strength allows for general
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegories"} actegories
            instead of just the canonical action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            on itself (see below).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#abstractly Abstractly
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %strong left-strong monad
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              in the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mtext -
                    %mi Act
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\text{-}Act
              of left
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegory"} actegories
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
          %p
            The classical definition of left-strength is on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            itself, regarded as equipped with the canonical
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            The extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            that a left-strong monad has as opposed to the underlying monad on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong strength
          %p
            Dually, a
            %strong right-strength
            is the analogous concept where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is instead acting on the right.
          %p
            %strong Details:
          %p
            If we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}V
            for the one-object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            once, we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mtext -
                  %mi Act
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Lax
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Funct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V\text{-}Act \simeq Lax2Funct(\mathbf{B}V, Cat)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on the right we have the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax 2-functors
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}V
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            of and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
          %p
            The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            defines a canonical functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat V : \mathbf{B}V \to Cat
          %p
            The left-strong monad, being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            in this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            is given by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a lax
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo :
                        %mover
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : \hat V \to \hat V
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    unit:
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi η
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi Id
                            %mi V
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta : Id_V \Rightarrow T
                %li
                  %p
                    product:
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mo :
                          %mi T
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi T
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu : T \circ T \Rightarrow T
            %li
              %p satisfying the usual uniticity and associativity constraints.
          %p
            By the general logic of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Ck%29-transformation"} transformations
            the components of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            are themselves a certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %p
            Then the
            %a{:href => "#ConcreteDefinition"} usual diagrams
            that specify a strong monad
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                unitalness and functoriality of the component functor of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %li
              %p naturalness of unit and product modifications.
          %h3#ConcreteDefinition Concretely
          %p
            A
            %em left-strong monad
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes,1)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T, \eta, \mu)
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            of the form
          #eq:LeftStrengthNaturalTrafo.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  t_{A,B}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  A \otimes T B
                  \\longrightarrow
                  T(A\otimes B)
                  \\,,
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em left-strength
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagrams commute
