                  %use{:x => "163.278138", "xlink:href" => "#OzFjDHmzmpwrTqik5msLa29oBDQ=-glyph5-1", :y => "68.635034"}
          %p#LinearMonads
            The notion of strong monads for which the (left) strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LeftStrengthNaturalTrafo"} (1)
            is even a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            has been called
            %em very strong
            (
            %a{:href => "#Ratkovic13"}> Ratkovic (2013), Def. 3.1.6
            ) or
            %em linear
            (
            %a{:href => "#BergerRatkovic19"}> Berger &amp; Ratkovic (2019), §1.2
            ).
          %p
            More generally, if a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            acts on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bullet : V\times C\to C
          %p
            then a
            = succeed "-strength" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a family of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo •
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo •
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_{A,B}:A\bullet T(B)\to T(A\bullet B)
            satisfying similar commutative diagrams.
          %p
            In particular, the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            of a strong monad is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+functor"} strong functor
          %h3#rightstrength Right-strength
          %p
            A
            %strong right-strength
            for the monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_{A,B}:T A \otimes B \to T(A\otimes B)
            satisfying analogous diagrams to those for the left-strength.
          %p
            %strong Remark.
            In some papers, the term “strength” is used for what is here called a “left-strength” and the term “costrength” is used for what is here called a “right-strength”. However, this terminology is confusing, as it is not in line with the usual convention in category theory of prefixing a term by “co-” when it is the same concept in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+monoidal+category"} reversal of the tensor product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi rev
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes^{rev} B = B\otimes A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+costrength"} tensorial costrength
            for more details.
          %p
            A
            %strong strength
            (on a monoidal category which is not necessarily symmetric) is a left-strength and a right-strength such that the two induced maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X\otimes T Y) \otimes Z \to T(X\otimes (Y\otimes Z))
            agree. If the category is symmetric monoidal, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
            if it is equipped with a left-strength (hence also a right-strength) such that the two induced maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \otimes T Y \to T(X\otimes Y)
            agree.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Every monad on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            is canonically left-strong (see
            %a{:href => "#example-in-set"} below
            for why).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The left-strength for the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list+monad"} list monad
                works in the following way. Given sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ↦
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (x,[y_1,\dots,y_n]) \; \mapsto \; [(x,y_1),\dots,(x,y_n)] .
              %p
                In other words, it turns an element of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and a list of elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                into a list of elements of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            %li
              %p
                For the left
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+monad"} action monad
                (on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                or on any other
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
                as follows (see
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "#Brandenburg2014"} Brandenburg ‘14, Example 6.3.4
          %svg{:height => "28.19pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 235.271 28.19", :width => "235.271pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
