          %ul
            %li
              %p
                On a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative
                precisely if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative as a monoid
                (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Brandenburg2014"} Brandenburg ‘14, Example 6.3.12
            %li
              %p
                For
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                is the map
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ↦
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (x,p) \;\mapsto\; \delta_x\otimes p
              %p
                for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in P Y
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative
                and it allows us to easily define the
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    product of measures
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/product+of+measures"} ?
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meas"} Meas
                has another
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+structure"} monoidal structure
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a{:href => "#sato"} Sato ‘16
          %h2#interaction_with_the_kleisli_category Interaction with the Kleisli category
          .query
            %p Is what’s written here somehow the same as the abstract definition? Can we be more general than the Kleisli category and pick an arbitrary adjunction?
          %p
            Given a monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M : C \to C
            on a monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, I)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(M)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J : C \to Kl(M)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Obj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in Obj(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Obj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] \in Obj(Kl(M))
            for the corresponding object in the Kleisli category. Moreover, given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi MY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to MY
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f] : [X] \to [Y]
          %p Then there is a correspondence between
          %ul
            %li
              left-strengths of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %li
              functorial operations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot : C \times Kl(M) \to Kl(M)
              that are
              %ul
                %li
                  left unital:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mi A
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \odot A \cong A
                  (naturally),
                %li
                  right unital:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi JX
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \odot [I] \cong JX
                  (naturally),
                %li
                  mixed associative:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \otimes Y) \odot [Z] \cong X \odot (Y \odot [Z])
                  (naturally),
                %li
                  subject to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+laws"} coherence laws
                  (see at
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+of+a+monoidal+category"} action of a monoidal category
          .query
            %p
              Right unitality entails that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \odot [Y] \cong X \otimes Y \odot [I] \cong [X \otimes Y]
              so basically
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is fixed on objects. Is it better to fix this on the nose? Does this violate the
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %p
            This correspondence is used in the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/call-by-push-value"} call-by-push-value
            as an adjoint logic.
          %p
            Indeed, assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is left-strong. Then we define
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \odot [Y] := [X \otimes Y]
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                        %mo ′
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ′
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{X \to X'} \odot [g]^{[Y] \to [Y']} := [t_{X', Y'} \circ (f \otimes g)]
          %p This preserves
          %ul
            %li identity because the left-strength commutes with the unit,
            %li composition because the left-strength commutes with monad multiplication.
          %p This is
          %ul
            %li left unital on objects (by mapping the left unitor into the Kleisli category),
            %li naturally so because the left-strength is compatible with the left unitor,
            %li right unital on objects (by mapping the right unitor into the Kleisli category),
            %li naturally so because the left-strength commutes with the unit (!),
            %li mixed associative on objects (by mapping the assiciator into the Kleisli category),
            %li naturally so because the left-strength is compatible with the associator.
          %p The coherence laws will be satisfied.
          %p
            Conversely, assume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
            is given. Then we define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi MY
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi MY
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi JMY
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi MY
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi JY
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [t_{X, y}] : J(X \otimes MY) \cong X \otimes MY \odot [I] \cong X \odot JMY \xrightarrow{1_X \odot [1_{MY}]} X \odot JY \cong X \otimes Y \odot [I] \cong J(X \otimes Y).
          %p This is natural and
          %ul
            %li
              commutes with the unit because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              preserves identity:
