          %p
            In other words, functions into a monoid form canonically a monoid under
            %em pointwise addition
            (or multiplication).
          %h2#moggis_typing_rules_and_parameterized_definition Moggi’s typing rules and parameterized definition
          %p
            Moggi (
            %a{:href => "#Moggi89"}> Moggi ‘89
            ) proposed the following typing rules for a sequence operator:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi let
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi in
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash t:X}{\Gamma\vdash \eta(t):T(X)}
                  \\qquad
                  \\frac{\Gamma,x:X \vdash u: T(Y)\quad \Delta\vdash t:T(X)}
                  {\Gamma,\Delta\vdash let\,x=t\,in\,u:T(Y)}
          %p To interpret these rules in a category, where types are interpreted as objects and judgements are interpreted as morphisms, we require
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For each object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X)
            %li
              %p
                a morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\eta_X:X\to T(X)
                for each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                (equivalently, a natural family of functions
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\Gamma,X)\to C(\Gamma, T(X))
            %li
              %p
                a family of functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *_{\Gamma,\Delta}:C(\Gamma\otimes X, T(Y))\times C(\Delta,T(X))\to C(\Gamma\otimes \Delta,T(Y))
                natural in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %p such that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %mi η
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f * \eta =f
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta * f=f
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %mo *
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h*(g*f)=(h*g)*f
          %p With the subscripts:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo *
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f *_{\Gamma,X} \eta_X = f
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta *_{I,\Delta} f=f
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi Ξ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Ξ
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi X
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo *
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Ξ
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h *_{\Gamma,\Delta\otimes \Xi} (g *_{\Delta,\Xi} f) = (h *_{\Gamma,\Delta\otimes X} g) *_{\Gamma\otimes\Delta,\Xi} f
          %p
            Such a structure is the same thing as a strong monad. One way to see this is to notice that it is essentially the same as the Kleisli triple form of an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+monad"} enriched monad
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            regarded with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            monoidal structure. More concretely,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo *
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{X\otimes Y}*_{X,T(Y)} id_{T(Y)}:X\otimes T(Y)\to T(X\otimes Y)
          %p is a left-strength map.
          %h2#uniqueness_of_leftstrength_with_enough_points Uniqueness of left-strength with enough points
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Moggi)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a category with finite products and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be a left-strong monad on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:1\to X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:1\to T(Y)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mo !
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    t\circ(x,y)\ = \
                    T((x\circ !_Y),id_Y)\circ y\ : 1 \to T(X\times Y)
            %p
              Hence if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(1,-):C\to Set
              is faithful, then there is at most one left-strength for any ordinary monad on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            In other words, a monad being left-strong is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
            in a category with enough points.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+strength"} tensorial strength
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/costrong+monad"}> costrong monad
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotensorial+strength"} cotensorial strength
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monad"} monoidal monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+monad"} enriched monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of strong monads was introduced (as an alternative description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+monads"} enriched monads
            and related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monads"} monoidal monads
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monads"} commutative monads
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monads on symmetric monoidal closed categories
                %strong 21
                (1970) 1-10 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01220868"}> doi:10.1007/BF01220868
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#kock71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Closed categories generated by commutative monads
                %strong 12
                4 (Nov 1971) 405-424 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S1446788700010272"}> doi:10.1017/S1446788700010272
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/KockMonoidalMonads.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#kock72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong functors and monoidal monads
                %strong 23
                (1972) 113–120 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01304852"}> doi:10.1007/BF01304852
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://tildeweb.au.dk/au76680/SFMM.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                H. Lindner,
                %em Commutative monads
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Deuxiéme colloque sur l’algébre des catégories
            %li#Keigher78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Keigher"}> William Keigher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symmetric monoidal closed categories generated by commutative adjoint monads
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/numdam-bin/fitem?id=CTGDC_1978__19_3_269_0"} NUMDAM
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1978__19_3_269_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The notion of strong monads is picked up in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
          %ul
            %li#Moggi91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"}> Eugenio Moggi
                , Def. 3.2 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Notions of computation and monads
                %strong 93
                1 (1991) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(91)90052-4"}> doi:10.1016/0890-5401(91)90052-4
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.disi.unige.it/person/MoggiE/ftp/ic91.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Jean Goubault-Larrecq, Slawomir Lasota, David Nowak,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Logical Relations for Monadic Types
                %strong 18
                6 (2008) 1169-1217 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0511006"}> arXiv:cs/0511006
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129508007172"} doi:10.1017/S0960129508007172
          %p More resources and surveys:
          %ul
            %li#Seal12
              %p
                Gavin J. Seal,
                %em Tensors, monads and actions
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0101"}> arXiv:1205.0101
                )
            %li#Brandenburg2014
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Brandenburg"}> Martin Brandenburg
                ,
                %em Tensor categorical foundations of algebraic geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.1716"}> arXiv:1410.1716
                )
            %li#McDermottUustalu22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dylan+McDermott"}> Dylan McDermott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarmo+Uustalu"} Tarmo Uustalu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What Makes a Strong Monad?
                %strong 360
                (2022) 113-133 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.00851"}> arXiv:2207.00851
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.360.6"} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.360.6
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Perrone"}> Paolo Perrone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Starting Category Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.worldscientific.com/worldscibooks/10.1142/13670"} website
          %p Usually strong monads are described explicitly in terms of data and coherence conditions. The above repackaging of the definition in terms of a 2-category of actions appears in the comments of the blog post:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em The Monads Hurt My Head – But Not Anymore
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/07/the_monads_hurt_my_head_but_no.html#c025476"}> blog
              )
          %p More in-text references:
          %ul
            %li#sato
              Tetsuya Sato,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Giry monad is not strong for the canonical symmetric monoidal closed structure on Meas
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.05939"} arXiv:1612.05939
          %p The terminology of “very strong” monads:
          %ul
            %li#Ratkovic13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kruna+S.+Ratkovic"}> Kruna S. Ratkovic
                , Def. 3.1.6 in:
                %em Morita theory in enriched context
                (2013) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2774"}> arXiv:1302.2774
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Daniel Schmitter, Def. 8.10 in:
                %em On Operations with Binders and Operations with Equations
                (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://researchportal.bath.ac.uk/en/studentTheses/on-operations-with-binders-and-operations-with-equations"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://purehost.bath.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/210538297/ThesisCorrectionsDanielSchmitter.pdf"} pdf
          %p also called “linear monads” in:
          %ul
            %li#BergerRatkovic19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clemens+Berger"}> Clemens Berger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kruna+Ratkovic"} Kruna Ratkovic
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gabriel-Morita theory for excisive model categories
              %strong 27
              (2019) 23–54 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.03863"}> arXiv:1705.03863
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10485-018-9539-x"} doi;10.1007/s10485-018-9539-x
        .revisedby
